{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792740000000-engineer-f1b-receiver-custody-state.prompt.md\ncat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-14 18:45:42 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-15 01:45:42 +07\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: pending (provider-generated; capture thread.started.thread_id after dispatch)\n# Task: F1b, lower-task must not inherit a receiver's custody/close/gate state (production fix + test; UNCOMMITTED; the Architect reviews the uncommitted diff before any commit)\nRole: VM Runtime\nImplementers:\n- Model: gpt-6.1-sol | Assigned: 2026-10-15 01:45:42 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: owner routing to codex; GPT family so the reviewer is the Claude Architect\nWorktree /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p8f1b, branch srvm-phase8-f1b from srvm-phase8 (3d626385). Read ./collab/architect-f1-review2.md IN FULL (Fable Architect, finding R2 and its evidence table and the exact prescription) and ./collab/f1-report.md. THE DEFECT (Architect ruling, with a probe): `restore-task` (src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc ~2083-2267, `base` at ~2090-2091 and the fenced child spawn) starts from the receiver and keeps its :yin.k/closes, :yin.k/issued (non-exclusive lower), :yin.k/gate, :yin.k/custody and stale :yin.k/recovery-waits/-parked/-cell-ids; the deleted v1 test asserted \"Receiver-local close/issue state is removed\" and the old `isolated-receiver` (git show 67b98907:src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc ~3425-3433) dissoc'd exactly these. THE FIX (Architect prescription): `base` starts from (dissoc recv :yin.k/closes :yin.k/issued :yin.k/gate :yin.k/custody :yin.k/recovery-waits :yin.k/recovery-parked :yin.k/recovery-cell-ids) and the same for the spawned child; then `with-custody` sets what the lower decides (fork: none; exclusive: issued 0, gate :running; fenced: gate :exporting etc.). Do NOT touch :modules (the composition-owned registry stays; the Architect documents it as intended until the owner rules on linked entries) and do NOT change :resources merge. TESTS (in test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc, extending `lowering-isolates-task-state-from-a-populated-receiver`, or a sibling deftest): poison those seven keys on the populated receiver; on the fork lower assert (not (contains? restored k)) for each AND (= (select-keys clean ks) (select-keys restored ks)); add one exclusive lower using rows_custody_test's grant helpers asserting (empty? (:yin.k/closes m)) and (= 0 (:yin.k/issued m)) and the gate/custody the exclusive path sets; one fenced lower asserting the recovery-* keys are the lower's own; add the registry line: (is (some? (module/resolve-module (:modules restored) 'receiver.mod)) \"the composition's registry is the receiver's\"). Check driver.cljc (~1565, 2085-2107) and holder/*.cljc callers that read these keys from the lowered machine to confirm nothing relied on inheriting them: run `grep -rn \":yin.k/closes\\|:yin.k/issued\\|:yin.k/gate\\|:yin.k/recovery\" src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf` and explain each reader in the report; if a caller DID rely on inheritance, STOP and report (design question for the Architect). Portability: .cljc JVM/Node/Dart rules (no for over long seqs, no private var access, (assoc {} ...), :cljd FIRST in any reader conditional). Verification, foreground JVM: kondo on changed files; clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-custody-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test, then EVERY yin.vm.ucf.* namespace (-e :slow, and -i :slow with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 for the Stage E rows and compose-rows slow tests; npm ci first if a Node peer is needed). No existing root id may move. MUTANTS (cp backup; diff and git diff --quiet to verify): revert each dissoc key one at a time; each must fail the new test. DO NOT COMMIT or stage; do not touch test/yang/. Report collab/<ms>-engineer-f1b.report.md beginning with Completed-GMT/Completed-Local/Coding-Agent/Session-ID: the diff summary, the reader analysis, counts, mutants. End READY.\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/architect-f1-review2.md collab/f1-report.md docs/agents/build-n-test.md\n git status --short\nsed -n '2060,2290p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\nrg -n 'lowering-isolates|grant|fenced' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/architect-f1-review2.md collab/f1-report.md docs/agents/build-n-test.md\n git status --short\nsed -n '2060,2290p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\nrg -n 'lowering-isolates|grant|fenced' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"Completed-Local: 2026-10-15 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude (claude-fable-5-1), Architect, second pass\nBrief: collab/1792730000000-architect-f1-review2.prompt.md\nTarget: worktree datomworld-srvm-p8f1 @ f9d15d36 + uncommitted rows_test.cljc (`lowering-isolates-task-state-from-a-populated-receiver`, 88 assertions)\n\n## Part 1: the test, read against the deleted tests\n- Not vacuous: every \"absent\" assertion is paired with `(= clean restored)` on the same field, and every field is first shown to differ between the populated receiver and the clean lower (`not=`), so an empty-collection pass is impossible. This is stronger than the deleted `a-poisoned-receiver-behaves-as-a-clean-one`, which compared code only and used `not (contains?)` elsewhere.\n- Install child: the whole child VM is compared (`:installs` equality), plus the seven fields at the child's path; the resume to 42 proves the child's stream is the task's. Covers the deleted `v1-child-state-and-receiver-isolation` child half.\n- Resource contract: the collision case pre-places the receiver's stream at exactly the key the clean task mints and asserts it is neither overwritten nor reused. This is the `:yin.k/k-1`-style collision my first pass (R4) asked for; the author had it already.\n- No masking: `:make-stream nil` is the fenced-spawn discipline rows.cljc itself uses, so no constructor noise is hidden; no relift, no normalization, no `#?`, no private access, no `for` over a long seq. Portable by inspection; Node/Dart lanes not run by me (JVM only, per the brief).\n- What it does NOT claim, correctly: the author report scopes out \"other receiver composition keys\". Part 2 shows that scope hides a defect.\n\n## Part 2: R2 with evidence (temporary probe, deleted; src untouched)\nReceiver poisoned with `:yin.k/closes [{:stream-id :yin.k/k-9 :yin.k/issue 5}]`, `:yin.k/issued 99`, `:yin.k/gate :running`, `:yin.k/custody {:poison true}`, `:yin.k/recovery-waits/-parked/-cell-ids`, and two registry entries (`receiver.mod` addressed, `receiver.local` address-less). Survivors after `rows/lower-task`:\n\n| lower | closes | issued | gate | custody | recovery-* | registry (both entries) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fork (reader, install root) | KEPT | KEPT 99 | KEPT :running | KEPT | KEPT (stale) | resolvable |\n| exclusive (grant) | KEPT | reset 0 | :running (overwritten) | overwritten | KEPT (stale) | resolvable |\n| fenced | KEPT | reset 0 | :exporting | KEPT {:poison true} | overwritten | resolvable |\n| fork install child | absent | absent | absent | absent | absent | resolvable (spawn-child copies the registry) |\n\nRuling:\n- `:yin.k/closes`: LEAK. Consumers key closes by `:stream-id` (holder/reader.cljc:224 defers reads, writer.cljc:225/255 emits closes, engine.cljc:602 resolves them); `fresh-key!` avoids only `(keys (:resources base))`, so a receiver close on `:yin.k/k-9` names whatever task stream later mints k-9. A lowered task would defer reads and emit closes for streams it never closed.\n- `:yin.k/gate` and `:yin.k/issued` on the fork path: LEAK. A fresh receiver's fork lowers with gate nil (rows_custody_test:257); into a receiver gated `:running` the same task queues its closes instead of performing them (engine.cljc:752), and into one gated `:ended` every close throws \"Gate is closed\". Task behaviour depends on the prior receiver.\n- `:yin.k/custody` (fork, fenced) and stale `:yin.k/recovery-*` (non-fenced): LEAK, lower impact (export.cljc:472 and the driver read these; a stale custody record on a fork is a false provenance claim).\n- Module registry: INTENDED composition-owned state (primitives, stream module, effect handlers; `check-dependencies!` resolves primitives against it; `spawn-child` propagates it on purpose). The old `isolated-receiver` dropped addressed (linked) entries and kept address-less ones; whether a linked `receiver.mod` should stay visible to a lowered task is an owner/linker question (linker owns modules). Not a blocker; document it.\n- `:resources` merge: intended (R3 stands; collision case proves disjointness).\nThis is a regression of a pinned contract: the deleted v1 test asserted \"Receiver-local close/issue state is removed\" and the old `isolated-receiver` dissoc'd exactly these keys; phase 8d deleted the test and rows.cljc never carried the dissoc. Production impact today is nil only because driver.cljc:1565 lowers into the config-supplied receiver, never a run one.\n\n## Part 3: mutants over restore-task (each applied alone, src restored byte-exact, `git diff --quiet -- src` after every one; test diff re-verified identical to collab/f1-uncommitted.diff; baseline rerun 88/0/0)\n| mutant | result | killing assertions |\n|---|---|---|\n| keep `(:parked recv)` | killed: 5 F | `:parked equals a fresh receiver's lower` (x2), `(not (contains? (:parked restored) :receiver-only))`, child field |\n| keep `(:store recv)` | killed: 5 F | same shape on `:store` |\n| keep `(:installs recv)` | killed: 12 F 2 E | `:installs` equality, child's stream/cursor keys missing, resume cannot append |\n| conj receiver waits onto `entries` | killed: 8 F 2 E | `:wait-set` equality, `not-any? :receiver-only`, stream-key, resume wakes with a diagnostic |\n| keep receiver `:code`/`:code-aliases` | killed: 6 F | `:code` equality root+child (and `:wait-set`, segment aliases shift) |\n| seed `used` with fixed keys only (reuse receiver key) | killed: 2 F | collision case: `(= receiver-stream (get (:resources collision) stream-key))`, `collision-stream-key` not in receiver |\n\n## Findings\n| id | sev | file:line | finding | fix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R2 | P2 | src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:2090-2091, 2222-2237 | `restore-task` starts from `recv` with its `:yin.k/closes`, `:yin.k/issued`, `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/custody`, `:yin.k/recovery-waits/-parked/-cell-ids` intact; `with-custody` only overwrites some of them on the exclusive/fenced paths (table above). Pinned contract lost at 8d. | Own src slice F1b: `base` starts from `(dissoc recv :yin.k/closes :yin.k/issued :yin.k/gate :yin.k/custody :yin.k/recovery-waits :yin.k/recovery-parked :yin.k/recovery-cell-ids)` (same for the spawned child), then `with-custody` sets what the lower decides. Add to this test: poison those seven keys on `populated`; assert `(not (contains? restored k))` for each on the fork lower, `(= (select-keys clean ks) (select-keys restored ks))` for the same keys; one exclusive lower (rows_custody_test's grant helpers) asserting `(empty? (:yin.k/closes m))` and `(= 0 (:yin.k/issued m))`. Document the registry: `(is (some? (module/resolve-module (:modules restored) 'receiver.mod)) \"the composition's registry is the receiver's\")` until the owner rules on linked entries. |\n| R3 | P3 | rows.cljc:2240 docstring | unchanged from first pass: \"recv holds no link wait of its own\". | one sentence |\n| R6 | P3 | collab engineer report line 26 | \"No receiver-isolation defect found\" is a statement about the seven fields only; the custody keys were scoped out, not checked. | Ledger entry pointing at R2. |\n\n## Verdict\nSIGN-OFF, scoped: this test closes F1 as specified (seven fields, resources, child, resume) and is landable as is; it is not vacuous and kills all six mutants. R2 is a src defect outside this test's claim, fixed in its own slice F1b with the assertions listed above; F1b joins F2 as a gate before `srvm-phase8` merges to master. Node/Dart lanes for this namespace remain to be run at that landing.\nSIGN-OFF\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-14 18:33:05 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-15 01:33:05 +07\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a13baa-4e66-71b2-8355-62da1bad118f\n# F1 receiver isolation — QA & Verification\n\nImplemented UNCOMMITTED on `srvm-phase8-f1`, base `f9d15d36`. Nothing staged or committed. No production change remains; no `test/yang/` file touched.\n\n## Test\n\nAdded one portable `.cljc` deftest, `lowering-isolates-task-state-from-a-populated-receiver`, in `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:1089` (88 assertions).\n\n- Blocked reader built with rows-support AST/machine helpers: defines `reader-owned` as 7, then blocks on its first source read. After feeding the source, it returns \"B\".\n- Install parent from `support/parked-installer`: its live `host.mod` child waits on a source read, then defines the exported function. After feeding the child source, the parent finishes with 42.\n- Both packs come from `support/lift`. Each is lowered into a fresh receiver and into a receiver with its own loaded code, store, module store, parked record, install record, wait entry, ready queue and stream resource.\n- Assert exact equality of `:code`, `:store`, `:module-stores`, `:parked`, `:installs`, `:wait-set`, and `:ready-queue`, plus explicit absence of receiver-only entries. The install comparison includes the whole child VM; the seven fields are also compared directly at the child's path. The receiver fields are asserted different from the clean lower before checking replacement.\n- Shared descriptor attachments make the install VM records directly comparable. `:make-stream nil` prevents construction of unrelated call streams during lowering; `:attach-stream` supplies the same composition attachment operation for subsequent module completion. No normalization, relift, private var access, or host-specific branch masks field differences.\n- Resume both clean and populated lowers after feeding their waiting source, assert halted state and the expected value. An additional reader lower occupies the exact resource key the clean task uses, asserts allocation around it, and also resumes to \"B\".\n\n## Resource contract\n\nResources are merged by design, not replaced wholesale. The tested populated receiver retains its exact resource handle at `vm/call-in-stream-key`. The reader collision receiver retains its handle at the clean task's usual stream key. The lowered reader's stream and cursor-cell keys are present in its resource table and disjoint from the receiver's keys; the collision case proves that a key already occupied by a receiver stream is neither reused nor overwritten by the task's fresh allocations. The install child likewise has its own restored stream/cursor resources and clean task state.\n\nMore precisely, `restore-task` reserves the fixed composition keys and the existing keys of the receiver resource table for each task it restores, then allocates fresh stream and cursor-cell keys in that task's resource scope. Child tasks are spawned into fresh VM state; this does not promise global key uniqueness across parent and child tables. Fixed link request/response aliases may be assigned carried handles when link waits are restored. Other receiver composition keys remain according to the existing implementation. This test does not claim that lowering removes all resources, resets every receiver key, or replaces its entire module registry.\n\nNo receiver-isolation defect found; production code was not changed.\n\n## Verification (foreground JVM)\n\n`clj -M:kondo --lint test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-custody-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test`: 88 tests, 2,136 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.\n\nFocused command: `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -v yin.vm.ucf.rows-test/lowering-isolates-task-state-from-a-populated-receiver`: 1 test, 88 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors, including a rerun after all mutants were restored.\n\nNode/Dart execution was not run; this task requested the JVM gate. The added test uses portable builders and public APIs, `(assoc {} ...)` for constructed maps, short vector iterations and no new reader conditionals.\n\n## Mutants\n\nA `cp` backup of `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` was taken at `/tmp/datom-f1-rows.cljc.backup`. Each mutant was applied separately to `restore-task`, run with the focused command, and restored with `cp` in a `finally` block. After every restoration, both `diff -u` against the backup and `git diff --quiet -- src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` passed.\n\n| Mutation | Result | Failing isolation assertion |\n|---|---|---|\n| Keep `(:parked recv)` instead of `parked'` | exit 1; 5 failures, 0 errors | `:parked equals a fresh receiver's lower`; stale receiver parked record survives |\n| Keep `(:store recv)` instead of `store'` | exit 1; 5 failures, 0 errors | `:store equals a fresh receiver's lower`; `receiver-only :wrong` survives |\n| Keep `(:installs recv)` instead of `installs'` | exit 1; 12 failures, 2 errors | `:installs equals a fresh receiver's lower`; receiver install survives and the required child is lost |\n\nThe install mutant's two follow-on errors are attempts to feed the now-missing child's stream; direct field assertions already fail independently. Mutant logs: `/tmp/datom-f1-mutant-parked.log`, `/tmp/datom-f1-mutant-store.log`, `/tmp/datom-f1-mutant-installs.log`.\n\n`git diff --check` passed; the index is unchanged. The final tracked diff is confined to `rows_test.cljc`; this report is a new untracked file. Pre-existing prompt, sign-off and session stdout files were left alone.\n\nREADY\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n?? collab/1792740000000-engineer-f1b-receiver-custody-state.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792740000000-engineer-f1b.codex.stdout.log\n?? collab/architect-f1-review2.md\n?? collab/f1-report.md\n\n\n(defn- spawn-child\n  \"A fresh install child of recv's composition (the engine's spawn\n   discipline): answers [child recv']. A fenced rehydration spawns it\n   without the stream constructor, so the child's construction creates no\n   stream (its default call pair): rehydration initializes nothing. The\n   constructor is the child's again afterwards.\"\n  [recv response mn fenced?]\n  (let [n (or (:origins recv) 0)\n        origin (keyword (str (name (or (:origin recv) :t0)) \".\" n))\n        registry (reduce (fn [r [m e]] (module/assoc-module r m (dissoc e :bindings)))\n                         (:modules recv) (module/module-entries (:modules recv)))\n        source (:secret-source recv)\n        child (module/spawn-module (cond-> recv fenced? (assoc :make-stream nil))\n                                   (:value (:image response))\n                                   {:modules registry :origin origin\n                                    :ancestry (conj (vec (:ancestry recv)) mn)\n                                    :capability-secret (when (fn? source) (source origin))})]\n    [(cond-> child fenced? (assoc :make-stream (:make-stream recv)))\n     (assoc recv :origins (inc n))]))\n\n\n(defn- restore-task\n  \"Step 12 for one task: code by A, fresh resource keys, values re-sealed\n   under recv, waits as engine entries, parked records, installs, and the\n   custody check-custody! answered (with-custody).\"\n  [recv task handles custody root?]\n  (let [[_ _ kind _ id-counter free-env store module-stores parked census _ code installs waits parked-id result]\n        (:slots task)\n        base (reduce (fn [r [_ v]] (module/attach-module r v))\n                     (assoc recv :code {} :code-aliases {} :program nil)\n                     (sort-by #(str (key %)) code))\n        aliases (:code-aliases base)\n        used (atom (into fixed-resource-keys (keys (:resources base))))\n        identities (sort-by str (distinct (map #(first (:slots %)) (rows-of task :stream true))))\n        stream-keys (into {} (map (fn [identity] [identity (fresh-key! used)])) identities)\n        pairs (mapv vec (partition 2 (:slots census)))\n        cell-keys (into {} (map (fn [[c _]] [c (fresh-key! used)])) pairs)\n        bodies (into {} pairs)\n        stream-key #(get stream-keys (first (:slots %)))\n        parked-index (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots parked)))\n        cache (atom {})\n        resolving (atom #{})]\n    (letfn [(value\n              [v]\n              (case (first v)\n                :lit (second v)\n                :cursor-cell (engine/issue-ref base :cursor-ref (get cell-keys (second v)))\n                :row (row (second v))))\n            (row\n              [n]\n              (let [ss (:slots n)]\n                (case (:kind n)\n                  :vec (mapv value ss)\n                  :list (apply list (map value ss))\n                  :set (into #{} (map value) ss)\n                  :map (into {} (map (fn [[k x]] [(value k) (value x)])) (partition 2 ss))\n                  :prim (vm/primitive-function (get (:primitives base) (first ss)))\n                  :stream (engine/issue-ref base :stream-ref (stream-key n))\n                  :closure (let [[a entry params bindings store-of] ss]\n                             (module/lower-closure base\n                                                   (cond-> {:yin.k/binding :named\n                                                            :yin.k/format :yin.semantic-register/code\n                                                            :yin.k/segment a :yin.k/entry entry :yin.k/params params\n                                                            :yin.k/env (keyed bindings)}\n                                                     (some? store-of) (assoc :yin.k/store-of store-of))\n                                                   identity))\n                  :reified (values/continuation (:owner base) (assoc (payload (first ss)) :type :reified-continuation))\n                  :parked-ref (parked-record (first ss)))))\n            (keyed\n              [n]\n              (into {} (map (fn [[k x]] [k (value x)])) (partition 2 (:slots n))))\n            (env\n              [n]\n              (let [[b store-of] (:slots n)]\n                (cond-> (keyed b) (some? store-of) (assoc engine/store-of-key store-of))))\n            (k-of\n              [n]\n              (mapv (fn [f]\n                      (let [[a pc e w rd] (:slots f)]\n                        {:type :return :segment (get aliases a) :pc pc :env (env e) :window (keyed w) :rd rd}))\n                    (:slots n)))\n            (payload\n              [n]\n              (if (= :tail (:kind n))\n                (let [[a site k] (:slots n)]\n                  {:segment (get aliases a) :site site :k (k-of k) :deliver {:deliver :return}})\n                (let [[a pc e w k rd] (:slots n)]\n                  {:segment (get aliases a) :pc pc :env (env e) :window (keyed w) :k (k-of k)\n                   :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd rd}})))\n            (parked-record\n              [p]\n              (or (get @cache p)\n                  (do (when (contains? @resolving p)\n                        (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :non-canonicalizable {:yin.k/hint :parked-cycle}))\n                      (swap! resolving conj p)\n                      (let [r (assoc (payload (get parked-index p)) :type :parked-continuation :id p)]\n                        (swap! cache assoc p r)\n                        (swap! resolving disj p)\n                        r))))\n            (cell-stream\n              [c]\n              (stream-key (first (:slots (get bodies c)))))\n            (entry\n              [w]\n              (let [[state pending] (:slots w)\n                    [reason & xs] (:slots pending)\n                    p (payload state)]\n                (case reason\n                  :next (assoc p :reason :next :stream-id (cell-stream (first xs))\n                               :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref :id (get cell-keys (first xs))})\n                  :ffi (let [[c call-id op] xs]\n                         (cond-> (assoc p :reason :next :stream-id (cell-stream c) :call-id call-id\n                                        :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref :id (get cell-keys c)})\n                           (some? op) (assoc :op op)))\n                  :put (let [[s v op-id] xs]\n                         (cond-> (assoc p :reason :put :stream-id (stream-key s) :datom (value v))\n                           (some? op-id) (assoc :op-id op-id)))\n                  :ffi-request (let [[call-id envelope req resp c op-id] xs\n                                     datom (value envelope)]\n                                 (cond-> (assoc p :reason :put :stream-id (stream-key req) :datom datom\n                                                :request-sent true :call-id call-id :op (apply2/request-op datom)\n                                                :response-cursor (get cell-keys c) :response-stream (stream-key resp))\n                                   (some? op-id) (assoc :op-id op-id)))\n                  (:link-request :link-response)\n                  (let [[link-id mn _ _ c envelope op-id] xs]\n                    (cond-> (assoc p :reason reason :link-id link-id :name mn\n                                   :request module/link-request-resource :response module/link-response-resource\n                                   :cursor (second (:slots (get bodies c))))\n                      (= :link-request reason) (assoc :envelope envelope)\n                      (some? op-id) (assoc :op-id op-id)))\n                  :install (assoc p :reason :install :name (first xs)))))]\n      (let [store' (reduce (fn [s [k v]] (engine/store-put s k (value v))) {} (partition 2 (:slots store)))\n            stores (into {} (map (fn [ms]\n                                   (let [[mid s] (:slots ms)]\n                                     [mid (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [k (value v)])) (partition 2 (:slots s)))])))\n                         module-stores)\n            parked' (into {} (map (fn [[p _]] [p (parked-record p)])) parked-index)\n            entries (mapv entry waits)\n            link (some #(let [[reason _ _ req resp] (:slots (second (:slots %)))]\n                          (when (contains? #{:link-request :link-response} reason) [req resp]))\n                       waits)\n            [base installs'] (reduce (fn [[r acc] inst]\n                                       (let [[mn phase parent response child] (:slots inst)\n                                             [spawned r'] (spawn-child r response mn (:fenced? custody))]\n                                         [r' (assoc acc mn {:phase phase :parent parent :response response\n                                                            :vm (restore-task spawned child handles custody false)})]))\n                                     [base {}] installs)\n            fe (keyed free-env)\n            resources (merge (:resources base)\n                             (into {} (map (fn [[identity k]] [k (get handles identity)])) stream-keys)\n                             (into {} (map (fn [[c body]]\n                                             [(get cell-keys c) {:stream-id (stream-key (first (:slots body)))\n                                                                 :cursor (second (:slots body))}]))\n                                   pairs)\n                             (when-let [[req resp] link]\n                               {module/link-request-resource (get handles (first (:slots req)))\n                                module/link-response-resource (get handles (first (:slots resp)))}))\n            ;; a fenced rehydration carries the counter exactly, so the\n            ;; relift reproduces the published root; a resumption adopts\n            ;; max(local, carried)\n            machine (cond-> (assoc base :store store' :module-stores stores :parked parked' :installs installs'\n                                   :resources resources :env fe :free-env fe :window {} :k nil :control nil\n                                   :ready-queue []\n                                   :id-counter (if (:fenced? custody)\n                                                 id-counter\n                                                 (max (or (:id-counter base) 0) id-counter)))\n                      ;; resource key -> the carrier's cursor cell, so one\n                      ;; cell is one key and two cells two (UCF 7.7.4)\n                      (:fenced? custody)\n                      (assoc :yin.k/recovery-cell-ids (into {} (map (fn [[c k]] [k c])) cell-keys)))]\n        (with-custody\n          (case kind\n            :halted (assoc machine :halted? true :blocked? false :value (value result) :wait-set [])\n            :parked (assoc machine :halted? true :blocked? false :value (get parked' parked-id) :wait-set entries)\n            (assoc machine :halted? false :blocked? true :value :yin/blocked :wait-set entries))\n          custody kind root?)))))\n\n\n(defn lower-task\n  \"The lower of §7.2 into recv, a fresh semantic-register machine of the\n   receiver's composition: gate to frames (decode) and the store keys,\n   then pendings and install records, dependency closure (code, closures,\n   primitives), each step over the whole tree before the next, then\n   custody (check-custody!), all before the first attach! (step 11); then\n   restore (step 12), which therefore meets no carrier defect. Answers the\n   restored machine, or throws the first refusal as ex-data.\n   opts: :budget; :exclusive, refuse a fork root; :grant and :protection,\n   required by a blocked or parked exclusive root (handoff.cljc's reader\n   contract: the grant names :checkpoint, the pack address, its lease,\n   holder, tenure and evidence; :protection maps every stream identity of\n   the tree to its class); :fenced, an administrative rehydration that\n   skips the grant and restores every task of the tree gated :exporting,\n   its waits in :yin.k/recovery-waits and its parked records in\n   :yin.k/recovery-parked (none in the scheduler), its id counter the\n   carried one exactly and :yin.k/recovery-cell-ids mapping each cursor\n   cell's resource key to its carried cell id.\"\n  ([recv wire attach!] (lower-task recv wire attach! {}))\n  ([recv wire attach! opts]\n   (let [task (decode wire (merge default-budget (:budget opts)))]\n     (when-not (= :task (:kind task)) (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :root {}))\n     ;; each step over the whole tree before the next (§3.3)\n     (doseq [step [check-stores! check-pendings! check-dependencies!]\n             [t role] (task-tree task :root)]\n       (step recv t role))\n     (let [custody (check-custody! task wire opts)]\n       (restore-task recv task (attach-streams! attach! task) custody true)))))\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:1089:(deftest lowering-isolates-task-state-from-a-populated-receiver\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:6:   :exclusive, runs the version-1 grant checks on a blocked or parked\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:9:   root with no grant gated :ended, and every refusal comes before the\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:90:(defn- grant-for\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:91:  \"A grant whose every part agrees with wire's custody: lease-1 to\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:124:  {:grant (grant-for wire enrolled) :protection (protection-for wire enrolled)})\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:138:(deftest an-exclusive-root-lowers-under-its-grant\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:145:            :input {:next 0 :prefix (get-in (grant-for wire #{\"s0\"}) [:evidence :yin.k/prefix])}\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:168:(deftest every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:171:        g #(update good :grant %)\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:174:            [[\"no grant\" (dissoc good :grant) :yin.k/awaiting-grant]\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:175:             [\"no evidence\" (g #(dissoc % :evidence)) :yin.k/awaiting-grant]\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:176:             [\"evidence still awaiting\" (ev #(assoc % :yin.k/status :yin.k/awaiting-grant)) :yin.k/awaiting-grant]\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:197:    (testing \"a malformed grant is a defect, not a refusal\"\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:199:            (rows/lower-task (s/receiver) wire (:attach! (s/attacher)) (assoc good :grant :x))))\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:212:             (:yin.k/status (refused wire (assoc-in good [:grant :evidence :yin.k/enrolled] #{}))))))\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:239:(deftest a-halted-exclusive-root-lowers-ended-with-no-grant\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:260:(deftest an-exclusive-blocked-root-under-fenced-skips-the-grant\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:261:  (let [[m _] (lowered @successor {:fenced true})]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc\nsed -n '239,295p' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc\nsed -n '1,55p' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\nsed -n '1080,1270p' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\nrg -n ':yin.k/(closes|issued|gate|custody|recovery)' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf\nrg -n 'with-custody' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc\nsed -n '239,295p' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc\nsed -n '1,55p' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\nsed -n '1080,1270p' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\nrg -n ':yin.k/(closes|issued|gate|custody|recovery)' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf\nrg -n 'with-custody' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.vm.ucf.rows-custody-test\n  \"Phase 7a: custody on rows (phase-7 plan §3.1). The lift under a header\n   writes the root's custody slot by kind and refuses :op-seq-exhausted,\n   :unprotected-pending and an id on an unenrolled stream; the lower's\n   custody stage runs the custody grammar, refuses a fork under\n   :exclusive, runs the version-1 grant checks on a blocked or parked\n   exclusive root, protection in both directions and op-id presence equal\n   to enrollment, restores the counter exactly, lowers a halted exclusive\n   root with no grant gated :ended, and every refusal comes before the\n   first attach (zero attachments). The version-1 rules these re-pin are\n   lift_v1_test's and handoff_v2_custody_test's, kept until phase 8.\"\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-fixtures :as f]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-support :as s]))\n\n\n(defn- custody-of\n  [wire]\n  (nth (:slots (rows/decode wire)) 16))\n\n\n(defn- identities\n  \"Every stream identity of the pack's tree.\"\n  [wire]\n  (into #{} (keep (fn [r] (when (= :stream (second r)) (nth r 2)))) (:yin.k/rows wire)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The lift under a header\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(deftest the-lift-writes-the-custody-slot-by-kind\n  (testing \"a first park: the exclusive policy, no origin\"\n    (is (= {:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence s/occurrence\n            :yin.k/arbitration s/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq 0}\n           (custody-of (s/lift (s/parked-reader) (s/header 0 nil #{}))))))\n  (testing \"a successor carries its origin\"\n    (is (= {:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence s/occurrence :yin.k/origin s/origin\n            :yin.k/arbitration s/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq 1}\n           (custody-of (s/lift (s/parked-explicit) (s/header 1))))))\n  (testing \"a halted root carries the origin alone\"\n    (is (= {:yin.k/origin s/origin} (custody-of (s/lift (s/halted-machine) (s/header 0 s/origin #{}))))))\n  (testing \"the enrolled set is a lift input that never travels\"\n    (let [wire (s/lift (s/with-op-id (s/parked-writer) :put (s/op-id 0)) (s/header 3 s/origin #{\"s0\"}))]\n      (is (not (contains? (custody-of wire) :yin.k/enrolled)))))\n  (testing \"no header is the fork: nil, on every kind\"\n    (doseq [m [(s/parked-reader) (s/parked-explicit) (s/halted-machine)]]\n      (is (nil? (custody-of (s/lift m nil))))))\n  (testing \"an install child's slot is nil\"\n    (let [task (rows/decode (f/base \"installs\"))]\n      (is (some? (nth (:slots task) 16)))\n      (is (nil? (nth (:slots (f/child task 'host.mod)) 16))))))\n\n\n(deftest the-lift-refuses-what-custody-forbids\n  (testing \"a counter at 2^52-1 on a blocked root\"\n    (is (= {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable :yin.k/kind :op-seq-exhausted :yin.k/next-op-seq rows/max-exact}\n           (s/lift (s/parked-reader) (s/header rows/max-exact s/origin #{})))))\n  (testing \"a halted root ignores the counter\"\n    (is (nil? (:yin.k/status (s/lift (s/halted-machine) (s/header rows/max-exact s/origin #{}))))))\n  (testing \"a retained write on an enrolled stream without an id\"\n    (is (= {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable :yin.k/kind :unprotected-pending :dao.stream/identity \"s0\"}\n           (s/lift (s/parked-writer) (s/header 3 s/origin #{\"s0\"})))))\n  (testing \"an id on a stream that is not enrolled\"\n    (is (= {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied :dao.stream/identity \"s0\"}\n           (s/lift (s/with-op-id (s/parked-writer) :put (s/op-id 0)) (s/header 3 s/origin #{})))))\n  (testing \"the same rules in an install child\"\n    (let [m (assoc-in (s/parked-installer) [:installs 'host.mod :vm] (s/parked-writer))]\n      (is (= :unprotected-pending (:yin.k/kind (s/lift m (s/header 6 s/origin #{\"s0\"}))))))\n    (let [m (assoc-in (s/parked-installer) [:installs 'host.mod :vm]\n                      (s/with-op-id (s/parked-writer) :put (s/op-id 4)))]\n      (is (= {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied :dao.stream/identity \"s0\"}\n             (s/lift m (s/header 6 s/origin #{}))))))\n  (testing \"a halted root with a header but no origin is refused, not downgraded\"\n    (is (= {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/kind :missing-header :yin.k/path [:custody :yin.k/origin]}\n           (select-keys (s/lift (s/halted-machine) (s/header 0 nil #{})) [:yin.k/status :yin.k/kind :yin.k/path]))))\n  (testing \"the lift runs the inspector's grammar before emitting\"\n    (is (= :op-seq-range (:yin.k/kind (s/lift (s/with-op-id (s/parked-writer) :put (s/op-id 3))\n                                              (s/header 3 s/origin #{\"s0\"})))))\n    (is (= :fork-op-id (:yin.k/kind (s/lift (s/with-op-id (s/parked-writer) :put (s/op-id 0)) nil))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The lower's custody stage\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- grant-for\n  \"A grant whose every part agrees with wire's custody: lease-1 to\n   holder-a at epoch 0, live tenure, an empty input prefix.\"\n  [wire enrolled]\n  (let [c (custody-of wire)\n        o (:yin.k/occurrence c)]\n    {:checkpoint (rows/pack-address wire)\n     :dao.lease/lease \"lease-1\" :dao.lease/holder \"holder-a\"\n     :tenure {:live true :now 0 :bound 10}\n     :evidence {:yin.k/status :yin.k/ready\n                :yin.k/binding {:yin.k/occurrence o :dao.lease/lease \"lease-1\" :dao.lease/holder \"holder-a\"\n                                :yin.k/transaction {:yin.k/arbitration (get-in c [:yin.k/arbitration :dao.stream/identity])}\n                                :yin.k/epoch 0}\n                :yin.k/prefix {:yin.k/occurrence o :dao.lease/lease \"lease-1\" :yin.k/frontier 0 :yin.k/inputs []}\n                :yin.k/enrolled enrolled}}))\n\n\n(defn- protection-for\n  [wire enrolled]\n  (into {} (map (fn [i] [i (if (contains? enrolled i) :enrolled :at-least-once)])) (identities wire)))\n\n\n(defn- lowered\n  \"[machine-or-refusal attaches] of wire lowered into a fresh receiver.\"\n  [wire opts]\n  (let [{:keys [attach! count]} (s/attacher)\n        r (try (rows/lower-task (s/receiver) wire attach! opts)\n               (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n                 (or (ex-data e) (throw e))))]\n    [r @count]))\n\n\n(defn- exclusive-opts\n  [wire enrolled]\n  {:grant (grant-for wire enrolled) :protection (protection-for wire enrolled)})\n\n\n(defn- refused\n  \"The refusal of wire lowered under opts, asserting zero attachments.\"\n  [wire opts]\n  (let [[r n] (lowered wire opts)]\n    (is (zero? n) (str \"refused before any attachment: \" (pr-str (select-keys r [:yin.k/status :yin.k/kind]))))\n    r))\n\n\n(def ^:private successor (delay (f/base \"successor\")))\n\n\n(deftest an-exclusive-root-lowers-under-its-grant\n  (let [wire @successor\n        [m n] (lowered wire (exclusive-opts wire #{\"s0\"}))]\n    (is (pos? n))\n    (is (= {:yin.k/occurrence s/occurrence :dao.lease/lease \"lease-1\" :dao.lease/holder \"holder-a\"\n            :yin.k/epoch 0 :yin.k/arbitration s/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq 3\n            :protection {\"s0\" :enrolled}\n            :input {:next 0 :prefix (get-in (grant-for wire #{\"s0\"}) [:evidence :yin.k/prefix])}\n            :tenure {:bound 10}}\n           (:yin.k/custody m))\n        \"custody from the evidence, the counter the slot's exactly\")\n    (is (= :running (:yin.k/gate m)))\n    (is (= 1 (:yin.k/issued m)))\n    (let [put (first (filter #(= :put (:reason %)) (:wait-set m)))]\n      (is (= 0 (:yin.k/issue put)) \"retained writes are numbered in carried order\")\n      (is (= (s/op-id 0) (:op-id put)) \"the id travels verbatim\"))\n    (testing \"the restored task relifts to the same root under the same header\"\n      (is (= (:yin.k/root wire)\n             (:yin.k/root (rows/lift-task (dissoc m :yin.k/custody :yin.k/gate :yin.k/issued) (s/serve-table)\n                                          (s/lift-opts (s/header 3 s/origin #{\"s0\"})))))))))\n\n\n(deftest the-counter-is-restored-exactly\n  (doseq [k [0 3 7 (dec rows/max-exact)]]\n    (let [wire (f/edit-custody @successor #(assoc % :yin.k/next-op-seq (inc k)))\n          wire (f/edit-task wire (fn [t] (f/update-put t #(f/set-op-id % (s/op-id k)))))\n          [m _] (lowered wire (exclusive-opts wire #{\"s0\"}))]\n      (is (= (inc k) (get-in m [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/next-op-seq])) (str k)))))\n\n\n(deftest every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment\n  (let [wire @successor\n        good (exclusive-opts wire #{\"s0\"})\n        g #(update good :grant %)\n        ev #(g (fn [x] (update x :evidence %)))]\n    (doseq [[label opts status]\n            [[\"no grant\" (dissoc good :grant) :yin.k/awaiting-grant]\n             [\"no evidence\" (g #(dissoc % :evidence)) :yin.k/awaiting-grant]\n             [\"evidence still awaiting\" (ev #(assoc % :yin.k/status :yin.k/awaiting-grant)) :yin.k/awaiting-grant]\n             [\"evidence unavailable\" (ev #(assoc % :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied)) :yin.k/unsatisfied]\n             [\"no input prefix\" (ev #(dissoc % :yin.k/prefix)) :yin.k/unsatisfied]\n             [\"an unreadable prefix\" (ev #(assoc-in % [:yin.k/prefix :yin.k/status] :yin.k/unsatisfied)) :yin.k/unsatisfied]\n             [\"another checkpoint: the root id is not the pack address\"\n(deftest a-halted-exclusive-root-lowers-ended-with-no-grant\n  (let [[m n] (lowered (f/base \"halted-root\") {})]\n    (is (zero? n))\n    (is (vm/halted? m))\n    (is (= 42 (vm/value m)))\n    (is (= :ended (:yin.k/gate m)))\n    (is (nil? (:yin.k/custody m)))))\n\n\n(deftest a-fork-is-refused-under-exclusive\n  (let [wire (s/lift (s/parked-reader) nil)]\n    (is (= {:yin.k/status :yin.k/profile-mismatch :yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive}\n           (refused wire {:exclusive true})))\n    (testing \"and lowers as a fork without it: no custody, no gate\"\n      (let [[m n] (lowered wire {})]\n        (is (pos? n))\n        (is (vm/blocked? m))\n        (is (nil? (:yin.k/custody m)))\n        (is (nil? (:yin.k/gate m)))))))\n\n\n(deftest an-exclusive-blocked-root-under-fenced-skips-the-grant\n  (let [[m _] (lowered @successor {:fenced true})]\n    (is (= :exporting (:yin.k/gate m)))\n    (is (nil? (:yin.k/custody m)))\n    (is (empty? (:wait-set m)) \"its waits are outside the scheduler\")\n    (is (= [:put] (mapv :reason (:yin.k/recovery-waits m))))))\n\n\n(deftest the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment\n  ;; continuation §11 Q3 (owner, 2026-10-13: lands in 7b): the response's\n  ;; link id is the row's parent, and its image is the one the manifest\n  ;; names, through the manifest's :yin.semantic/code derivation record\n  (let [m (s/parked-installer)\n        response (get-in m [:installs 'host.mod :response])\n        other (s/linked-response (s/def! 'f (s/lam [] (s/lit 7))) 'host.mod #{'f})\n        lift-with (fn [f] (s/lift (update-in m [:installs 'host.mod] f) nil))\n        kind (fn [inst-f]\n               (let [r (refused (lift-with inst-f) {})]\n                 [(:yin.k/status r) (:yin.k/kind r) (:yin.k/name r)]))]\n    (testing \"an honest response lowers\"\n      (let [[r n] (lowered (s/lift m nil) {})]\n        (is (pos? n))\n        (is (vm/blocked? r))))\n    (testing \"a response of another link\"\n      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-link-id 'host.mod]\n             (kind #(assoc-in % [:response :yin.link/id] [:other 9])))))\n    (testing \"no parent\"\n      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-link-id 'host.mod]\n             (kind #(assoc % :parent nil)))))\n    (testing \"no derivation record\"\n      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-image 'host.mod]\n             (kind #(update % :response dissoc :derivation)))))\n    (testing \"a derivation record the manifest does not name\"\n      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-image 'host.mod]\n             (kind #(assoc-in % [:response :derivation] (:derivation other))))))\n    (testing \"a changed record with the honest input and output\"\n(ns yin.vm.ucf.rows-test\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.jing.cbor-fixtures :as fx]\n            [yang.python.antlr.int-contract-fixtures :as ints]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-support :as support]\n            [dao.test-slow :as slow]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]))\n\n\n(defn error\n  [f]\n  (try (f) nil (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e (ex-data e))))\n\n\n(defn task-node\n  [cursor-cells result]\n  (rows/node :task [3 rows/profile :halted :root 0 (rows/node :bindings [])\n                    (rows/node :store []) [] (rows/node :parked-table [])\n                    (rows/node :cursor-cells (mapcat identity cursor-cells)) {} {} [] [] nil result nil]))\n\n\n(defn cursor-cell-body\n  [stream position]\n  (rows/node :cursor-cell-body [(rows/node :stream [stream {:peer stream}]) position]))\n\n\n(defn fixture\n  [a b x y]\n  (let [payload [:row (rows/node :map\n                                 [[:lit 'both] [:row (rows/node :set [[:cursor-cell x] [:cursor-cell y]])]\n                                  [:lit 'one] [:cursor-cell x]\n                                  [:lit 'streams] [:row (rows/node :set [[:row (rows/node :stream [a {:peer a}])]\n                                                                         [:row (rows/node :stream [b {:peer b}])]])]\n                                  [:lit 'cursors] [:row (rows/node :map [[:cursor-cell x] [:lit :x] [:cursor-cell y] [:lit :y]])]])]\n        task (task-node [[x (cursor-cell-body a 0)] [y (cursor-cell-body b 0)]] [:lit nil])]\n    (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots task) 6 (rows/node :store ['payload payload])))))\n\n\n(declare portable-frame)\n\n\n(defn- encoded\n  [x]\n  (cond (and (map? x) (contains? x :yin.k/row)) [:row (:yin.k/row x)]\n        (and (map? x) (contains? x :yin.k/cursor-cell)) [:cursor-cell (:yin.k/cursor-cell x)]\n        :else [:lit x]))\n\n\n      (let [pack (support/lift machine nil)\n            restored (rows/lower-task (support/new-machine) pack (fn [_] nil))]\n        (is (vm/halted? restored))\n        (is (= (encoded expected) (encoded (vm/value restored))))))\n    (let [fixture (ints/read-file)]\n      (doseq [n ints/int-names]\n        (is (= (ints/column fixture n \"cbor\") (encoded (ints/value n))) n)))))\n\n\n(deftest lowering-isolates-task-state-from-a-populated-receiver\n  (doseq [[label source path expected]\n          [[:reader (vm/run (support/load-ast\n                              (support/new-machine)\n                              (support/then\n                                (support/def! 'reader-owned (support/lit 7))\n                                (support/next-of\n                                  (support/cursor-of {:type :stream/make :buffer 4})))))\n            [] \"B\"]\n           [:install-child (support/parked-installer) [:installs 'host.mod :vm] 42]]]\n    (testing (name label)\n      (let [pack (support/lift source nil)\n            ;; Share attached handles so install records can be compared\n            ;; directly, including the child's entire VM. No constructor\n            ;; runs during either lower; the task's streams are attached.\n            handles (atom {})\n            attach! (fn [descriptor]\n                      (let [id (:dao.stream/identity descriptor)\n                            h (or (get @handles id)\n                                  (let [h (support/one-slot-stream id)]\n                                    (swap! handles assoc id h)\n                                    h))]\n                        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/handle h}))\n            fresh (support/receiver {:make-stream nil :attach-stream attach!})\n            receiver-stream (support/one-slot-stream \"receiver-only\")\n            ;; A fixed composition key is already reserved by lower-task,\n            ;; so adding it does not rename the task's fresh keys. This\n            ;; permits exact equality of all seven fields, without masking\n            ;; differences behind a relift or a normalization of VM state.\n            resource-key vm/call-in-stream-key\n            populated (assoc (support/load-ast fresh (support/lit :receiver-only))\n                             :store (assoc {} 'receiver-only :wrong)\n                             :module-stores (assoc {} 'receiver.mod (assoc {} 'x :wrong))\n                             :parked (assoc {} :receiver-only {:type :parked-continuation})\n                             :installs (assoc {} 'receiver.mod {:phase :running})\n                             :wait-set [{:reason :next :receiver-only true}]\n                             :ready-queue [{:receiver-only true}]\n                             :resources (assoc {} resource-key receiver-stream))\n            clean (rows/lower-task fresh pack attach!)\n            restored (rows/lower-task populated pack attach!)\n            fields [:code :store :module-stores :parked :installs :wait-set :ready-queue]\n            clean-task (get-in clean path)\n            restored-task (get-in restored path)\n            stream-key (:stream-id (first (:wait-set restored-task)))\n            cursor-key (get-in restored-task [:wait-set 0 :cursor-ref :id])\n            ;; Also occupy the exact key the clean task would use. This\n            ;; lower must allocate different keys, rather than overwrite\n            ;; or reuse the receiver's stream (including its cursor key).\n            collision-receiver (assoc populated :resources (assoc {} stream-key receiver-stream))\n            collision (when (= :reader label)\n                        (rows/lower-task collision-receiver pack attach!))\n            collision-task (get-in collision path)\n            collision-stream-key (:stream-id (first (:wait-set collision-task)))\n            collision-cursor-key (get-in collision-task [:wait-set 0 :cursor-ref :id])]\n        (is (seq (:code populated)) \"the receiver already owns code\")\n        (doseq [field fields]\n          (is (not= (get populated field) (get clean field))\n              (str field \" has receiver-only state to discard\"))\n          (is (= (get clean field) (get restored field))\n              (str field \" equals a fresh receiver's lower\"))\n          (is (= (get clean-task field) (get restored-task field))\n              (str field \" is isolated in the resumed task, including an install child\")))\n        (is (not (contains? (:store restored) 'receiver-only)))\n        (is (not (contains? (:module-stores restored) 'receiver.mod)))\n        (is (not (contains? (:parked restored) :receiver-only)))\n        (is (not (contains? (:installs restored) 'receiver.mod)))\n        (is (not-any? :receiver-only (:wait-set restored)))\n        (is (= receiver-stream (get (:resources restored) resource-key))\n            \"resource merging retains the receiver's composition stream\")\n        (is (not (contains? (:resources populated) stream-key)))\n        (is (not (contains? (:resources populated) cursor-key)))\n        (is (contains? (:resources restored-task) stream-key))\n        (is (contains? (:resources restored-task) cursor-key))\n        (when collision\n          (is (= receiver-stream (get (:resources collision) stream-key)))\n          (is (not (contains? (:resources collision-receiver) collision-stream-key)))\n          (is (not (contains? (:resources collision-receiver) collision-cursor-key))))\n        (is (= (:stream-id (first (:wait-set clean-task))) stream-key))\n        (is (= :next (:reason (first (:wait-set restored-task)))))\n        (is (empty? (:ready-queue restored-task)))\n        (doseq [m (cond-> [clean restored] collision (conj collision))]\n          (let [task (get-in m path)\n                _ (is (= :dao.stream/ok\n                         (:dao.stream/outcome\n                           (stream/append! (support/stream-of task) \"B\"))))\n                done (vm/run (vm/run m))]\n            (is (vm/halted? done))\n            (is (= expected (vm/value done))\n                \"the source read resumes to the same value in every receiver\")))))))\n\n\n(deftest result-only-values-complete-the-census\n  (doseq [[f expected] [[(fn [a _b] [a a]) 1]\n                        [(fn [a b] [a b]) 2]]]\n    (let [pack (support/lift (support/halted-with-cursors f) nil)\n          {:keys [attach! count]} (support/attacher)\n          recv (rows/lower-task (support/new-machine) pack attach!)\n          [a b] (vm/value recv)]\n      (is (= expected @count))\n      (is (= expected (clojure.core/count (filter #(= :cursor-cell-body (second %)) (:yin.k/rows pack)))))\n      (is (= (= 1 expected) (= a b)))))\n  (doseq [m [(vm/run (support/load-ast (support/new-machine)\n                                       (support/lam [] (support/lit 1))))\n             (support/halted-result-closure)]]\n    (let [pack (support/lift m nil)\n          {:keys [attach! count]} (support/attacher)\n          recv (rows/lower-task (support/new-machine) pack attach!)]\n      (is (values/closure? (vm/value recv)))\n      (is (seq (:code recv)) \"the result-only closure carries its code\")\n      (when (seq (:module-stores m))\n        (is (= 2 @count))\n        (is (= #{'c 'd} (set (keys (get-in recv [:module-stores 'host.mod])))))))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:113:  {:yin.k/enrolled [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/target :yin.k/effect-kinds]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:114:   :yin.k/target-closed [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/target]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:115:   :yin.k/admitted [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/target :yin.k/op-id :yin.k/intent\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:117:   :yin.k/offered [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/id :yin.k/policy\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:122:   :yin.k/bound [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/occurrence :dao.lease/lease\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:125:   :yin.k/reclaimed [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/occurrence :dao.lease/lease\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:128:   :yin.k/refused [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/proposer :dao.lease/proposal]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:130:   :yin.k/fenced [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/op-id :yin.k/incarnation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:132:   :yin.k/quarantined [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/op-id]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:134:   :yin.k/input [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/occurrence :dao.lease/lease\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:137:   :yin.k/resumed [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/occurrence :dao.lease/lease\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:139:   :yin.k/completed [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/occurrence :dao.lease/lease]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:140:   :yin.k/succeeded [:yin.k/custody :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/successor\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:185:  \"The kind a fact dispatches on: its `:yin.k/custody`, or for a lease\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:188:  (let [c (get fact :yin.k/custody)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1349:      (seq (:yin.k/closes m)) :pending-close\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:2026